{"outcome":"DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY","best_model_id":"PGS002042","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Phenotype/endpoint fidelity (highest-priority factor per prs_model_evaluator preamble) is perfect and tied: both candidates explicitly target corneal dystrophy across trait_reported/trait_efo/phenotyping_reported. Study/transportability context is also tied: both come from the same UKB portability framework paper (“Portability of 245 polygenic scores…”) with the same training N (391,124), the same selected European validation ancestry and validation N (19,321), and similarly basic covariates (age/sex/PCs etc.), which the skill notes are not heavy leakage.

Given this near-clone setting, the performance-metrics guidance says to prefer the stronger PRS-comparable metric when available. The selected European validation record reports higher partial correlation for PGS002042 (partial-r=0.0471; CI 0.033–0.0611) than for PGS001835 (partial-r=0.0384; CI 0.0243–0.0525), supporting slightly better standalone genetic signal. As a secondary structural tie-breaker (lower priority than endpoint/metrics per the preamble), PGS002042 uses LDpred2 with far more variants (59,944 vs 38), which is a supportive robustness signal within the same publication family.

Heritability sanity-check: get_heritability_records("corneal dystrophy") returned no matching EUR h2 records, so no heritability ceiling/context can be applied; confidence remains moderate rather than high, but this does not negate the within-family partial-r advantage for PGS002042."}
